{
  "outcome": "resolved",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Mei, I looked into it. Your mailbox is 99.4% full, and that's why Outlook keeps warning you and some emails won't send. Once a mailbox is completely full, sending stops altogether. Here's how to free up space:\n\n1. Empty your Deleted Items and Junk Email folders. Right-click each one and choose Empty Folder.\n2. Sort your Inbox and Sent Items by size, largest first. Delete emails with big attachments you no longer need, or save the attachments to OneDrive first and then delete the emails.\n3. If you need to keep old mail, turn on Online Archive and move anything older than a year into it. This frees up space in your main mailbox without losing anything.\n\nAfter you clean up, it can take up to an hour for the mailbox size to update. Then try sending again. If you still get the warning, or you can't get the mailbox below about 95% and need more space for your work, reply to this ticket. We can ask for a larger mailbox, which needs your manager's approval.",
  "internal_note": "I checked the user's account: it's active, with no lockouts. The mailbox is 50,890 of 51,200 MB used (99.4%). That's above the 90% warning level and close to 100%, where sending stops, so it explains both the warnings and the failed sends. I gave the user the KB-010 cleanup steps: empty Deleted Items and Junk, delete or move large attachments to OneDrive, and turn on Online Archive for mail older than a year. I didn't queue any actions. If the mailbox is still over 95% after cleanup and her role needs more space, escalate to Tier 2 for a larger quota (needs manager approval).",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-010"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
